---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Sebastian Correa-Gallego">
  <header class="hero">
    <img class="hero__img" src="/assets/field.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" fetchpriority="high" />
    <div class="hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <h1 class="hero__name">Sebastian<br />Correa-Gallego</h1>
        <p class="hero__degree">B.Sc. in Biology</p>
        <p class="hero__phrase">
          On how microbial communities assemble — and whether the state they reach is
          predictable from the cells themselves.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <a class="hero__cue" href="#research" aria-label="Continue to research"><span aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
  </header>

  <section class="focus" id="research">
    <div class="wrap focus__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal">Research</p>

      <h2 class="focus__lead reveal">
        How does order arise from parts that were previously autonomous — and does that
        process follow rules, or depend on history?
      </h2>

      <div class="focus__grid">
        <div class="focus__text reveal">
          <p>
            It is most tractable in microbial community assembly, where one pool of organisms may
            converge on a single reproducible state or settle into any of several, depending on
            which arrives first. Ecology describes both outcomes; it cannot yet predict which one
            a given community will take.
          </p>
          <p>
            I ask whether that can be anticipated from the organisms themselves — from how a cell
            divides its finite proteome between growing quickly and growing efficiently, and how
            selection reshapes those strategies as a community assembles. Allocation is
            measurable. If its spread across a species pool sets when arrival order matters,
            contingency becomes a quantity rather than a caveat.
          </p>
          <p class="focus__coda">
            The question continues upward, toward the thresholds at which cooperating parts cease
            to be separable. The approach is experimental: cultivation, growth physiology, and
            quantitative analysis of community structure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="focus__figure reveal">
          <svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 340 258" role="img" aria-labelledby="fig-title">
            <title id="fig-title">
              One species pool resolving either into a single convergent end state, or into
              several alternative states set by the order in which its members arrive.
            </title>

            <text class="fig__label" x="170" y="10" text-anchor="middle">the same species pool</text>

            <g class="fig__pool">
              <circle cx="66"  cy="34" r="3.2" />
              <circle cx="96"  cy="24" r="3.2" />
              <circle cx="126" cy="36" r="3.2" />
              <circle cx="156" cy="25" r="3.2" />
              <circle cx="186" cy="35" r="3.2" />
              <circle cx="216" cy="24" r="3.2" />
              <circle cx="246" cy="35" r="3.2" />
              <circle cx="274" cy="26" r="3.2" />
            </g>

            <path class="fig__line" pathLength="1" d="M170,50 C 170,68 118,64 90,84" />
            <path class="fig__line" pathLength="1" d="M170,50 C 170,68 222,64 250,84" />

            <g>
              <path class="fig__line" pathLength="1" d="M90,84 C 48,118 54,178 90,204" />
              <path class="fig__line" pathLength="1" d="M90,84 C 112,114 68,170 90,204" />
              <path class="fig__line" pathLength="1" d="M90,84 C 132,118 126,178 90,204" />
              <circle class="fig__ring" cx="90" cy="208" r="11.5" />
              <circle class="fig__node fig__node--solid" cx="90" cy="208" r="6.5" />
              <text class="fig__label" x="90" y="242" text-anchor="middle">convergent</text>
            </g>

            <g>
              <path class="fig__line" pathLength="1" d="M250,84 C 226,118 212,172 208,204" />
              <path class="fig__line" pathLength="1" d="M250,84 C 254,118 244,172 248,204" />
              <path class="fig__line" pathLength="1" d="M250,84 C 274,118 288,172 292,204" />
              <circle class="fig__node" cx="208" cy="208" r="5" />
              <circle class="fig__node" cx="248" cy="208" r="5" />
              <circle class="fig__node" cx="292" cy="208" r="5" />
              <text class="fig__label" x="250" y="242" text-anchor="middle">contingent</text>
            </g>
          </svg>

          <figcaption class="fig__caption">
            One pool, two outcomes. Which of them a community reaches is the quantity I want to
            predict.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
